    ]
   },
   "section": "hook",
   "overlays": {
    "hook": "They said Black men *could not fly.*"
   },
   "captions": false,
   "speed": 1.25
  },
  {
   "id": "name",
   "text": "Meet the Tuskegee Airmen.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "title",
    "title": "The Tuskegee Airmen",
    "sub": "1941-1946"
   },
   "section": "who"
  },
  {
   "id": "study",
   "text": "In 1925, an Army War College study claimed Black men did not have what it takes to lead, or to fly.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "card",
    "image": "airman2",
    "caption": "A Tuskegee Airman, 1945"
   },
   "section": "story"
  },
  {
   "id": "start",
   "text": "In 1941, the Army started a test program at Tuskegee, Alabama. Thirteen men began the first class. Five finished.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "card",
    "image": "firstclass",
    "caption": "A class of Tuskegee cadets"
   },
   "overlays": {
    "pin": {
     "label": "Tuskegee",
     "sub": "Alabama"
    }
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "eleanor",
   "text": "That spring, First Lady Eleanor Roosevelt went up in a plane with their chief instructor.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "card",
    "image": "eleanor",
    "caption": "Eleanor Roosevelt and Chief Anderson, 1941"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "momyer",
   "text": "In 1943 they went to war. Their own commander reported they lacked discipline and aggressiveness.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "card",
    "image": "sq99",
    "caption": "Pilots of the 99th Fighter Squadron"
   },
   "section": "twist",
   "overlays": {
    "stamp": "1943"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "study2",
   "text": "A War Department study found the opposite. They had performed as well as the white squadrons.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "title",
    "title": "As well as",
    "sub": "the white squadrons, 1944"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "red",
   "text": "Then they painted their tails red, and escorted American bombers across Europe.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "title",
    "title": "The Red Tails",
    "sub": "332nd Fighter Group"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "berlin",
   "text": "In March 1945 they flew all the way to Berlin, and shot down three German jets.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "card",
    "image": "map",
    "caption": "Pilots studying the target, Italy"
   },
   "overlays": {
    "pin": {
     "label": "Berlin",
     "sub": "1945"
    }
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "arrest",
   "text": "That same spring in Indiana, more than a hundred Black officers were arrested, in a fight over an officers club for white men only.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "card",
    "image": "freeman",
    "caption": "Freeman Field, Indiana, 1945"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "truman",
   "text": "In 1948, President Truman ordered equal treatment in the armed forces.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "title",
    "title": "1948",
    "sub": "Executive Order 9981"
   },
   "section": "legacy"
  },
  {
   "id": "medal",
   "text": "In 2007, they received the Congressional Gold Medal.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "card",
    "image": "bush",
    "caption": "U.S. Capitol, 29 March 2007"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "end",
   "text": "They said Black men could not fly. So they flew.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "photo",
    "image": "airmen2",
